  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Severe thunderstorms, tornadoes and flash \nflooding expected across many areas tonight.\n\nA very warm and humid airmass across the area remain in place.\nMeanwhile, upper short waves will continue across PA/NJ into the\nevening. These factors will promote thunderstorms into the \nearly overnight period. Upper winds will contribute to severe \nthunderstorms in many areas with Watches issued for all areas \nuntil 8PM (most areas) and 10PM for south NJ and much of \nDelmarva. Damaging winds are the primary threat, but there could\nbe a few tornadoes as well, especially over southeastern \nPennsylvania, southern New Jersey and the Delmarva.\n\nThe Flood Watch remains up for the area and several Flash Flood\nWarnings have already been issued. When all is said and done, \nthe updated QPF forecast through Wednesday is around 2 to 4 inches\nof rain north of I-78, with 1 to 3 inches elsewhere. As is usual\nin summer convective setups, locally higher amounts are possible.\nHREF Max in northern New Jersey has highly localized amounts up\nto 8\" through Wednesday. Have multiple ways to receive any flood\nalerts. \n\nAs low pressure meanders, another front will come through on \nWednesday, capable of producing some heavy downpours yet again \nfor the northern third of the region. This will move over some \nmore urbanized areas in North Jersey that should get a decent \namount of rainfall today, potentially causing more problems. \nWhile mainstem river flooding is not currently forecast, some \nsmaller creeks and streams within the Passaic and Raritan basins\ncould see some rises that result in flooding. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There will be lingering chances for some showers and \nthunderstorms across portions of the area for Thursday and Friday \nhowever severe weather and flash flooding is not expected. \n\nLow pressure and its associated upper level trough will be slow\nto depart to our east through the course of Thursday and \nFriday. This will keep chances for showers and thunderstorms in \nthe forecast, especially for Thursday. For Thursday, forecast \nmodels continue to depict a closed circulation at 500mb slowly \ndrifting east through New England. Along the backside of this \nfeature, some showers and thunderstorms are likely to affect the\narea with the highest POPs (around 60 percent) for portions of \ncentral and NJ. In terms of timing, it should be mainly focused \naround the afternoon into the early evening period coincident \nwith peak heating. Generally expect highs ranging from the \nmiddle to upper 70s north to the low to mid 80s south. \n\nFor Friday, the system will continue to pull farther away so by and \nlarge it should be a partly to mostly sunny day with slightly warm \ntemperatures compared to Thursday. However still can't rule out some \nisolated showers and storms in the afternoon mainly over NJ into \nportion of eastern PA (mainly north of Philly). POPs though are only \naround 20 percent.",
